This is the furthest I've ever gotten without going into explore mode.
As usual,
I played a Ranger. Unusually, I picked a human ranger (and am Pissed at
rust
monsters now). Unlike most of my "good" games, I did not get any
wishes.
(Most of the time, the only way I get upto Sokoban was because of an
early
GDSM wish or something) However, I was RNG blessed with a massive 4x6
scroll shop with at least 5 scrolls of identify in it early in the
game. I saved them for after the Gnometown. Got at least 5 or 6 enchant
armor and weapon scrolls as well, brought them all down to test for
b/u/c status.

Found 20 daggers, but they were too heavy. Dropped most of them in a
stash
somewhere, enchanted and rustproofed 9.

Cleared mines, found 2 luckstones and 5 touchstones. (no flintstones or
loadstones).

I was again blessed with good armor. Found jumping boots off of
something,
got a +3 orc shield, gloves of power, +1 cloak of protection, dwarvish
mithril,
and a dented pot.

Finished the mines, picked up the first emerald I could find, then went
to Sokoban.

Got my amulet of reflection at Sokoban, got my first unicorn horn as
well as well
as poison resistance from the unicorn corpse.

Found an altar at lvl 9, sacrifest for 7 naked protection, returning to
Sokoban for a couple of jumps every time I got a spellbook. (made sure
my luck
was low enough to never get crowned). Hint for newbies out there: lots
of
luck =/= always equal good, especially if you don't want to be crowned.

Anyway, moved on, down and down the dungeon. Then, I found what I was
looking
for. A sleeping blue dragon in a throne room.

I cleared out the room, killed the dragon, used an enchant armor found
wayyy back
at the beginning and finally got my dragon scale mail. ( I was already
-10 AC, no
one could lay a finger on me. Figured to save the enchant armor for
better armors later).

-------------

A sidenote: I have found a strategy that when armed with loads of AC,
strong (throwing)  weapons and a unicorn horn, and dragon scale mail
of any kind makes identifying potions a very simple and risk-proof
task.

Simply find a sink, remove all armor except your dragon scale mail, and
start drinking. You'll get a plentitude of potions that just run out of
the faucet
literally, and most of the time, you can identify the potion that comes
out.

You take off your armor because of the chance you get a "freakish
metamorphasis". You leave your dragon scale mail on because you
get to turn into a big Dragon :-)

-----------

So anyway, I did that for a while. Turned into a dragon after about 50
quaffs
(expectedly. I had -6 or so AC without any of my other armor on. So I
felt safe with only my BDSM on). Then... I figured, shopkeeper killing
time!

I went back up, and combining the fact that I was:
1. A Blue Dragon
2. Had an Amulet of Reflection

It made facing shopkillers very very easy. I killed about 3 of them and
never droped below 100 hp. #monster lightning helps a lot. :-) Even
then,
shopkeepers starting hitting themselves with magic missle... you'd
guess that after the first time it was reflected, they'd stop ehh?

I was going for my 4th shopkeeper, and thats when I made my big
mistake.

I hit "down" twice, so I can move down a tunnel. First move, a yellow c

appears where I was about to go.

I hit "down" again, because well... I didn't realize it was there.

And I bit a cockatrice and died as a Blue Dragon :-(

Had all important intrinsics (speed, telepathy, poison resistance).
Level 3 magic cancelation, reflection, -13 AC before quest...

Tons of good items (9 blessed +1 rustproof daggers, amulet of
reflection,
a sack and bag of holding, 5+ identify scrolls and a bigger stash of
enchant weapons/armor scrolls) Jumping boots, gauntlets of power...

And even a bigger stash of polypile items, all on my Elbereth square
inside a box by my altar.

*Sigh* Thanks for hearing out my rant.

It just reminds me of how quickly this game could end, even when
_everything_ goes in your favor. It has to be a step-by step game...

I guess I just have to get used to polymorph before I start doing
my sink-quaffing strategy again.

